t1->t8 SCALING INVESTIGATION — 2026-08-08
=========================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P-cores + 4 E-cores (12 logical), macOS 26.5.2.
          THE CORE COUNT MATTERS: t=8 saturates the P-cores exactly, t=16
          oversubscribes onto E-cores. Any "why not 8x" reasoning that assumes
          12 uniform cores is wrong on this box.
Base      6c17d8c (main), default features, no -C target-cpu=native.
Method    Interleaved arms with rotating order inside every (vector, threads)
          cell, median of 5, min/max printed per cell. No `nice` on any timed
          run. Box was OTHERWISE IDLE for the ladder / shards / tracker A/Bs
          (scripts/perf/scaling_ab.sh). The dav1d gap sweep
          (scaling_gap_dav1d_2026-08-08.tsv) ran with ALLOW_LOAD=1 and a
          foreign process (qemu) above the 25% gate in 36 of 60 rows — its
          RATIOS are usable, its absolute ms are inflated. Tagged in column 9.
Harness   scripts/perf/scaling_ab.sh + scripts/perf/scaling_report.py (new),
          scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh (existing, two-point wall fit).

BASELINE REPRODUCES
-------------------
v4k_8tile 8bpc, ms/frame: t=1 367.14, t=4 116.52, t=8 71.34 -> 5.147x.
The campaign's standing figure is 5.15x. Same instrument, same answer.


1. THE DEFICIT IS WORK INFLATION, NOT IDLENESS
----------------------------------------------
The standing suspicion was the post-tile filter chain starving the pool. It is
not the main term. Per-worker busy time at t=8 is even to +-0.7% (62.5-63.4
ms/frame across 8 workers) and achieved occupancy is 7.143 of 8.

probe-tasktime, v4k_8tile 8bpc (scaling_taskprobe_2026-08-08.txt). CPU
ms/frame is tile_recon + the five filter stages; the probe's own two clock
reads per stage are inside both columns. Self-consistency check: predicted
wall = CPU / mean_active reproduces measured wall to within 1-2% at every
cell, which is what licenses using these as absolute numbers.

    t   CPU ms/frame   mean_active   CPU/occ   measured wall
    2      439.7         1.990/2      220.9       216.1
    4      457.1         3.917/4      116.7       115.7
    8      504.2         7.143/8       70.6        70.2

Same 136 tile-recon tasks and 34 filter-sbrow tasks at every thread count, so
the operation COUNT is fixed and only the per-operation cost moves: +14.7% CPU
from t=2 to t=8.

Deficit decomposition at t=8 against perfect 8x from t=1 (367.14/8 = 45.89):

    total deficit                              25.45 ms/frame
      work inflation  (504.2 - 367.1)/8        17.10 ms   67%
      lost occupancy  504.2/7.143 - 504.2/8     7.57 ms   30%
                                       sum     24.67 ms

**If CPU work did not inflate, the measured 7.143/8 occupancy alone would give
367.1/7.143 = 51.4 ms = 7.14x.** Occupancy is not the binding constraint.


2. THE INFLATION IS THE BORROW TRACKER
--------------------------------------
Wall-only interleaved A/B, tracker on (default) vs off (probe-untracked, which
disables tracking and leaves bounds checks alone), n=5, idle box
(scaling_tracker_ab_2026-08-08.tsv). Ranges do not overlap at any cell.

    t    plain    untracked   untracked/plain
    1   364.66     287.17        0.7875
    2   217.35     158.34        0.7285
    4   116.66      82.63        0.7083
    8    71.83      46.70        0.6501

    scaling t1->t8:  plain 5.077x   untracked 6.149x

The tracker is 27% of wall at t=1 and 35% at t=8 — its cost per access grows
with thread count while the access count is fixed. Removing it entirely buys
5.08x -> 6.15x, which is ~66% of the distance to dav1d's comparable model.

`sample`, 45 s steady state, self-time leaves, normalised to non-idle samples
(__psynch_cvwait excluded as parked-worker time):

    symbol                        t=2      t=8
    decode_coefs                47.17%   41.05%
    BorrowTracker::add<false>   11.91%   12.94%
    BorrowTracker::add<true>     7.83%    8.13%
    TinyLock::lock_slow          <0.38%    3.47%   <- the riser
    WithOffset (picture)         2.40%    3.81%
    DisjointMutGuard drop_glue   3.45%    3.49%

`lock_slow` is the contended spin path. It is invisible at t=2 and 3.47% of
non-idle CPU at t=8.

Why the collision exists, and why it is 4-way: the adaptive block shift gives
a 4K 8-bit plane shift 14 — the crate's own doc says "~4.3 picture rows per
block". v4k_8tile is 4 tile columns x 2 tile rows (136 tile tasks / 34 sbrows
= 4). All four concurrent workers in a tile row are inside the SAME block and
therefore the SAME shard. That also explains why t=4 -> t=8 is the weak step:
t=4 puts ~2 workers per tile row, t=8 puts 4.


3. THREE MEASURED NEGATIVES — DO NOT REPEAT
-------------------------------------------
(a) Finer blocks to separate the tile columns. CATASTROPHIC.
    scaling_blockshift_2026-08-08.tsv, n=5, idle, ratio to default at t=8:
        bs13 1.2014   bs10 1.8888   bs8 1.9111
    scaling t1->t8: default 5.147x, bs13 4.310x, bs10 2.754x, bs8 2.715x.
    At shift 10/8 a strided `block_mut` hull explodes into multi-shard and
    scaling collapses outright. The block shift is at a LOCAL OPTIMUM; the
    tile-column collision cannot be fixed by shrinking the block.

(b) 256 shards (new `shards-256` knob, added by this work, default off).
    scaling_shards_2026-08-08.tsv, n=5, idle, ratio to default:
        t=1 1.0730   t=4 1.0594   t=8 1.0295   (blockshift-14 + shards-256)
    Worse at every thread count. The larger table costs more than the
    collisions it removes. Shard count is also at a local optimum.
    Control arm blockshift-14 alone: t=1 1.0058, t=4 0.9998, t=8 0.9883.

(c) The wide path is NOT involved. probe-wide, 6 frames
    (scaling_widepath_2026-08-08.txt): wide_total = 0 at t=1, 2, 4 AND 8.
    #458's SHARDS_SERIAL=1 killed it at t=1 and it does not come back when
    ACTIVE_SHARDS rises to 128. multi-shard registrations are ~5,740/frame
    against ~50M adds. Any future plan that starts "the wide path at t=8"
    is starting from a false premise.

Already-known negatives re-confirmed as still-not-worth-retrying: TinyLock
backoff (null twice), block_mut held row guards (null), sub-SHARD_MIN_LEN
instances (the crate documents 1,027 small instances at ZERO contended
acquisitions; contention is 100% on the 12 picture planes).


4. CORRECTION TO THE TARGET: 6.83x, NOT 7.14x
---------------------------------------------
The 7.14x dav1d scaling figure is its DEFAULT configuration, which has frame
threading. Our checked build hard-pins n_fc=1 and structurally cannot do
that. Measured in one interleaved sweep, both dav1d arms present
(scaling_gap_dav1d_2026-08-08.tsv, LOADED — ratios only):

    scaling t1->t8:  ours 4.83x   dav1d --framedelay 1 6.83x   dav1d default 7.24x
    t=8 ratio to dav1d_fd1: ours 2.11x, dav1d_def 0.95x

The honest like-for-like target is dav1d's tile-only model at **6.83x**.
Frame threading is worth only 5% to dav1d at t=8 (34.6 vs 36.4 ms), so the
correction is real but small — 5.15x -> 6.83x is still a large deficit.


5. WHERE THE REMAINING 30% (THE OCCUPANCY TERM) IS
--------------------------------------------------
New instrument, `PROBE tailconc` / `tail_frac_of_wall` in
src/probe_tasktime.rs: the concurrency histogram restricted to samples where
NO worker is in a tile stage and at least one is in a filter stage — i.e. the
post-tile chain running alone.

    t    tail as frac of wall    mean active during tail
    2          0.0061                   1.00
    4          0.0108                   1.00
    8          0.1196                   3.92

The tail is 1.1% of wall at t=4 and **12.0% at t=8**. That is the occupancy
half of the t=4 -> t=8 step, located. Recovering it perfectly (8 active
instead of 3.92) is worth ~6% of t=8 wall, ~4.3 ms/frame.

CAVEAT ON THAT RUN: the TILE_ACTIVE counter this probe adds is itself
contended at t=8 and inflates the build's absolute stage ms (609 ms CPU /
7.039 = 86.5 ms predicted against ~71 ms real wall). The tail FRACTIONS are
ratios of sample counts and survive; do NOT quote absolute stage ms from a
build with this probe enabled. Use the unmodified probe-tasktime numbers in
section 1 for absolutes.

The serial spine is DeblockRows, gated in-order on
`frame_thread_progress.deblock`. dav1d has the identical rule, so the
structure is not the difference — the per-sbrow cost is.


NOT DONE / NEXT
---------------
No performance change shipped. The one principled fix left for the tracker
contention is issue #455's untested item: stop reserving the strided hull in
`block_mut`/`narrow_guard` and register per row-group instead. That is what
would let the block shift go fine enough to separate tile columns without the
(a) blowup. It is soundness-critical, it is adjacent to the March-2026 strided
tracker that was merged and reverted the next day, and it was not attempted
here rather than attempted and left unproven.
